    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef last_non_empty_string(s: str) -> str:\n    # Count frequency of each character\n    from collections import Counter\n    freq = Counter(s)\n    \n    # Find the maximum frequency\n    max_freq = max(freq.values())\n    \n    # Characters that appear max_freq times will be removed in the last operation\n    # We need to find the order in which they appear when scanned left to right\n    # Only keep characters that have frequency == max_freq, in order of first occurrence in the original string\n    \n    # Build result by iterating through s and keeping only characters that have freq == max_freq\n    # and ensuring we don't include duplicates in the result (but we want all occurrences that will be removed in the last step)\n    # Actually, we want the string right before the last operation\n    # In the last operation, we remove one occurrence of each character that has the maximum frequency\n    # So just before the last operation, each such character appears exactly max_freq times\n    \n    # But we don't need to simulate all operations - we can reason:\n    # After max_freq - 1 operations, each character that originally had frequency f will have frequency f - (max_freq - 1)\n    # So characters with original frequency max_freq will have frequency 1 at that point\n    # And they are the only ones that will be removed in the last operation\n    \n    # So the string right before the last operation consists of exactly one occurrence of each character that has max_freq\n    \n    # To get the order: we iterate through s and for each character with freq == max_freq, we add it to result\n    # and decrement a counter to avoid duplicates (since we only want one occurrence of each)\n    remaining = {char: 1 for char, count in freq.items() if count == max_freq}\n    \n    result = []\n    for char in s:\n        if char in remaining and remaining[char] > 0:\n            result.append(char)\n            remaining[char] -= 1\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
