    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_477",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array coins representing coins of different denominations and an integer k.\nYou have an infinite number of coins of each denomination. However, you are not allowed to combine coins of different denominations.\nReturn the k^th smallest amount that can be made using these coins.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: coins = [3,6,9], k = 3\nOutput:  9\nExplanation: The given coins can make the following amounts:\nCoin 3 produces multiples of 3: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, etc.\nCoin 6 produces multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18, 24, etc.\nCoin 9 produces multiples of 9: 9, 18, 27, 36, etc.\nAll of the coins combined produce: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, etc.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: coins = [5,2], k = 7\nOutput: 12 \nExplanation: The given coins can make the following amounts:\nCoin 5 produces multiples of 5: 5, 10, 15, 20, etc.\nCoin 2 produces multiples of 2: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, etc.\nAll of the coins combined produce: 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, etc.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= coins.length <= 15\n1 <= coins[i] <= 25\n1 <= k <= 2 * 10^9\ncoins contains pairwise distinct integers.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef kthSmallestAmount(coins: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n    # Remove duplicates (though problem states distinct, just to be safe)\n    coins = list(set(coins))\n    \n    # Remove coins that are multiples of smaller coins to optimize\n    # For example, if we have [2, 4, 8], we only need [2] since 4 and 8 are redundant\n    coins.sort()\n    reduced_coins = []\n    for coin in coins:\n        is_redundant = False\n        for existing in reduced_coins:\n            if coin % existing == 0:\n                is_redundant = True\n                break\n        if not is_redundant:\n            reduced_coins.append(coin)\n    coins = reduced_coins\n    \n    # If only one coin, answer is just coin * k\n    if len(coins) == 1:\n        return coins[0] * k\n    \n    # For multiple coins, use binary search + inclusion-exclusion principle\n    # Count how many amounts <= x can be formed\n    def count_amounts(x):\n        if x <= 0:\n            return 0\n        \n        count = 0\n        n = len(coins)\n        # Use inclusion-exclusion principle with bitmask\n        for mask in range(1, 1 << n):\n            # Compute LCM of selected coins\n            lcm_val = 1\n            bits = 0\n            valid = True\n            for i in range(n):\n                if mask & (1 << i):\n                    bits += 1\n                    lcm_val = lcm_val * coins[i] // math.gcd(lcm_val, coins[i])\n                    # If LCM exceeds x, no multiples will fit\n                    if lcm_val > x:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n            \n            if valid:\n                multiples = x // lcm_val\n                if bits % 2 == 1:  # odd number of coins - add\n                    count += multiples\n                else:  # even number of coins - subtract\n                    count -= multiples\n        \n        return count\n    \n    # Binary search for the k-th smallest amount\n    # Lower bound is 1, upper bound we estimate: worst case is coin with minimum value\n    min_coin = min(coins)\n    # Upper bound: k * min_coin (in worst case, all k amounts are multiples of min_coin)\n    # But could be less due to contributions from other coins\n    left, right = 1, k * min_coin\n    \n    while left < right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if count_amounts(mid) >= k:\n            right = mid\n        else:\n            left = mid + 1\n    \n    return left\n```",
