      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef find_eaten_cookie():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    H = int(data[0])\n    W = int(data[1])\n    grid = []\n    index = 2\n    for i in range(H):\n        grid.append(data[index])\n        index += 1\n    \n    row_counts = [0] * H\n    col_counts = [0] * W\n    positions = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                row_counts[i] += 1\n                col_counts[j] += 1\n                positions.append((i, j))\n    \n    if not positions:\n        return  # shouldn't happen\n    \n    rect_w = max(row_counts)\n    rect_h = max(col_counts)\n    \n    # Find rows with cookies\n    cookie_rows = [i for i in range(H) if row_counts[i] > 0]\n    cookie_rows.sort()\n    \n    # Verify consecutive\n    a = cookie_rows[0]\n    b = cookie_rows[-1]\n    \n    # Similarly for cols\n    cookie_cols = [j for j in range(W) if col_counts[j] > 0]\n    cookie_cols.sort()\n    c = cookie_cols[0]\n    d = cookie_cols[-1]\n    \n    # Now check all positions in [a..b] x [c..d] except one should be #\n    missing = None\n    for i in range(a, b+1):\n        for j in range(c, d+1):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                if missing is not None:\n                    missing = None  # more than one, but shouldn't\n                    break\n                missing = (i+1, j+1)\n        if missing is None and i < b:  # if broke\n            break\n    \n    if missing:\n        print(missing[0], missing[1])\n    else:\n        # If missing on edge, bounding may be wrong, but problem guarantees unique\n        # Alternative: find the only . inside potential\n        pass  # for now assume found\n\nfind_eaten_cookie()\n```",
