    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find substrings where:\n1. Each character appears exactly k times\n2. Adjacent characters differ by at most 2 in alphabet position\n\nLet me analyze the key insights:\n\n- For condition 1, if a substring has `t` distinct characters, its length must be exactly `t * k`\n- For condition 2, adjacent characters must have |pos(c1) - pos(c2)| ≤ 2\n\nSince the alphabet is small (26 letters), and for a valid substring of length `t*k`, we can iterate over possible numbers of distinct characters (t from 1 to 26), and use a sliding window approach.\n\nFor each possible number of distinct characters `t`:\n- Window length should be `t * k`\n- Use a sliding window of that length\n- Check if each character in the window appears exactly k times AND adjacent characters differ by at most 2\n\nWait, but the adjacent characters condition needs to be checked for every pair of adjacent characters in the substring, not just the window boundaries.\n\nLet me reconsider the approach:\n\n1. For each possible number of distinct characters `t` (1 to min(26, len(word)//k)):\n   - Window length = `t * k`\n   - Use sliding window of that length over the string\n   - For each window position, check:\n     a. Each character appears exactly k times in the window\n     b. For every adjacent pair in the window, |pos(c1) - pos(c2)| ≤ 2\n\n2. To check condition (b) efficiently, I can precompute where the \"bad\" boundaries are (positions where |word[i] - word[i+1]| > 2), and check if the window contains any bad boundary.\n\nHere's my approach:\n- Precompute an array `bad[i]` = 1 if |pos(word[i]) - pos(word[i+1])| > 2, else 0\n- Compute prefix sum of `bad` to quickly check if any bad boundary exists in a range\n- For each window, check if sum of bad in range [start, end-1] is 0 (no bad boundaries)\n- Also check character frequency condition\n\nSince k can be large and string length up to 10^5, and t only goes up to 26, this should be efficient.\n\n```python\ndef countCompleteSubstrings(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Convert characters to positions (0-25)\n    pos = [ord(c) - ord('a') for c in word]\n    \n    # Precompute bad boundaries: where adjacent chars differ by more than 2\n    bad = [0] * (n - 1)\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        if abs(pos[i] - pos[i + 1]) > 2:\n            bad[i] = 1\n    \n    # Prefix sum for bad boundaries\n    prefix_bad = [0] * (n)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        prefix_bad[i] = prefix_bad[i - 1] + (bad[i - 1] if i - 1 < len(bad) else 0)\n    \n    def count_bad(l, r):\n        # Count bad boundaries in range [l, r-1] (between positions l to r-1)\n        if l >= r:\n            return 0\n        return prefix_bad[r - 1] - prefix_bad[l]\n    \n    result = 0\n    \n    # Try each possible number of distinct characters (1 to 26)\n    for t in range(1, 27):\n        window_len = t * k\n        if window_len > n:\n            break\n        \n        # Sliding window of length window_len\n        # Track frequency count in current window\n        freq = [0] * 26\n        \n        # Initialize first window\n        for i in range(window_len):\n            freq[pos[i]] += 1\n        \n        # Check first window\n        if count_bad(0, window_len) == 0:\n            valid = True\n            for count in freq:\n                if count != 0 and count != k:\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n                # All non-zero counts should be exactly k\n            if valid and all(c == 0 or c == k for c in freq):\n                result += 1\n        \n        # Slide the window\n        for i in range(window_len, n):\n            # Remove leftmost character\n            freq[pos[i - window_len]] -= 1\n            # Add new character\n            freq[pos[i]] += 1\n            \n            # Check if window has bad boundaries\n            if count_bad(i - window_len + 1, i + 1) == 0:\n                valid = True\n                for count in freq:\n                    if count != 0 and count != k:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if valid and all(c == 0 or c == k for c in freq):\n                    result += 1\n    \n    return result\n```",
