e, we return 6.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1]\nOutput: 1\nExplanation:\nThere is a single subarray of nums which is [1], with its largest element 1. The first element is 1 and the last element is also 1.\nHence, we return 1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom typing import List\nimport bisect\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef countSubarrays(nums: List[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    left = [-1] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            left[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n    val_to_indices = defaultdict(list)\n    for i in range(n):\n        val_to_indices[nums[i]].append(i)\n    ans = 0\n    for r in range(n):\n        pos = left[r] + 1\n        indices = val_to_indices[nums[r]]\n        # count indices with pos <= idx <= r\n        left_bound = bisect.bisect_left(indices, pos)\n        right_bound = bisect.bisect_right(indices, r)\n        ans += right_bound - left_bound\n    return ans\n```",
