he closest occurrences.\n\nWait, let me think about the net effect. Each operation removes 2 or 3 characters total (left + right, or just left, or just right, or just the chosen one if we interpret differently - but the description says we delete the closest left and right, not the chosen one).\n\nRe-reading: \"Delete the closest occurrence of c to the left of i (if any) and the closest occurrence of c to the right of i (if any).\" So we delete 0, 1, or 2 other characters, but the character at position i remains.\n\nSo each operation keeps the character at position i, but may remove some other occurrences.\n\nThis means that for each character, as long as there are at least 2 occurrences, we can potentially reduce the count by removing some of them. But the question is: what's the minimum number we can get down to for each character?\n\nLet's think about character counts:\n- If a character appears only once, it can never be removed (since we need other occurrences to delete).\n- If a character appears multiple times, can we reduce it to 1 occurrence?\n\nFor \"aaabc\" (3 a's): yes, reduce to 1.\nFor \"cbbd\" (2 b's): yes, reduce to 1.\nFor \"dddaaa\" (3 d's, 3 a's): reduce each to 1.\n\nBut wait, what about a case like \"abcabc\"? Let's see:\n- a at index 0: no left, closest right a is at index 3, so we remove index 3 → \"abcbc\"\n- a at index 0: still no left, no right (a's only at 0 now), so stuck with 1 a\n\nActually, it seems like for each character, if it appears k times, we can reduce it to min(k, 1) occurrences? But that can't be right for all cases.\n\nWait, let me reconsider the operation. When I choose position i, I delete the closest left occurrence and closest right occurrence, but NOT the character at i itself.\n\nSo the character at position i survives the operation. If I have 3 occurrences of a character at positions p1 < p2 < p3:\n- If I choose p2, I delete p1 and p3, leaving only p2 → 1 occurrence\n- If I have 4 occurrences at p1, p2, p3, p4:\n  - Choose p2: delete p1 and p3, leaving p2, p4\n  - Now I have p2, p4. Choose p2: delete nothing to right (p4 is to the right but is it the closest? Yes, but the operation says delete the closest to the right, so p4 gets deleted, leaving only p2\n\nSo with 4 occurrences, I can reduce to 1.\n\nWith 5 occurrences p1,p2,p3,p4,p5:\n- Choose p3: delete p2 and p4, leaving p1,p3,p5\n- Choose p3: delete p1 and p5, leaving p3 → 1\n\nIt seems like for any character that appears at least once, I can reduce it to 1 occurrence if it appears at least once, but wait - if it appears exactly once, I can't do anything to it, so it stays at 1.\n\nBut what if I have an even number of occurrences? Let's try 2 occurrences at p1, p2:\n- Choose p1: no left, closest right is p2, delete p2 → left with p1 only\n- Choose p2: closest left is p1, no right, delete p1 → left with p2 only\n\nSo with 2 occurrences, I can reduce to 1.\n\nTherefore, for each character, if it appears at least once, I can reduce it to exactly 1 occurrence (unless there are 0 occurrences, but we're talking about characters that appear).\n\nBut wait, is this always possible? What about overlapping constraints? For example, if I have \"aabbaa\":\n- Characters: a(4 times), b(2 times)\n- Can I reduce all a's to 1 and all b's to 1? That would give length 2.\n\nLet's try:\n- Start with b at index 2: delete closest b to left (index 1) and closest b to right (none? indices: a=0,1; b=2,3; a=4,5). So closest b to left is 1, closest b to right is 3. Delete 1 and 3 → string becomes \"abaa\" (a0,b2,a3,a4)\n- Now with a at index 3: closest left a is 0, closest right a is 4. Delete 0 and 4 → string becomes \"ba\" → length 2.\n\nYes! So the pattern is clear: for each unique character, we can reduce it to exactly 1 occurrence, unless... \n\nWait, what about the constraint of the operation? Let me think of a tricky case: \"abacaba\" (a appears 4 times, b appears 1 time, c appears 1 time). We should be able to reduce to 3 (a,b,c).\n\nSo the minimum length should be the number of distinct characters in the string?\n\nLet me verify with the examples:\n- \"aaabc\": distinct characters = {a,b,c} = 3 ✓\n- \"cbbd\": distinct characters = {c,b,d} = 3 ✓\n- \"dddaaa\": distinct characters = {d,a} = 2 ✓\n\nThis matches all examples!\n\nThe reasoning: For any character that appears at least once, we can repeatedly use the operation to reduce its occurrences down to exactly 1. Characters that don't appear don't count. And operations on one character don't prevent us from reducing other characters.\n\nSo the answer is simply the number of distinct characters in the string.\n\n```python\ndef minimizedStringLength(s: str) -> int:\n    return len(set(s))\n```",
