bab\"+\"ab\"=\"ababab\")\n    #   i=3: \"abab\" + \"aba\" -> wait s[3:6]=\"bab\", s[:3]=\"aba\" -> \"bababa\"\n    #   i=4: s[4:6]=\"ab\", s[:4]=\"abab\" -> \"ababab\"\n    #   i=5: s[5:6]=\"b\", s[:5]=\"ababa\" -> \"bababa\"\n    #   So only i=0,2,4 give t.\n    #   So M = {2,4} (since i=0 excluded), count=2.\n    #   Now, from a non-t rotation, say i=1 (\"bababa\"), how many l such that rotation(\"bababa\", l) = \"ababab\"?\n    #       rotation(\"bababa\", l) = \"bababa\"[l:] + \"bababa\"[:l]\n    #       l=1: \"ababa\" + \"b\" = \"ababab\" = t — yes\n    #       l=2: \"baba\" + \"ba\" = \"bababa\" — no\n    #       l=3: \"aba\" + \"bab\" = \"ababab\" — yes\n    #       l=4: \"ba\" + \"baba\" = \"bababa\" — no\n    #       l=5: \"a\" + \"babab\" = \"ababab\" — yes\n    #       So 3 ways.\n    #   count=2, but we got 3.\n    \n    # Let's use the formula: \n    #   r = \"bababa\" = rotation(s,1)\n    #   We need rotation(s, 1+l) = t.\n    #   rotation(s, b) = t for b in {0,2,4}\n    #   So 1+l ≡ 0,2,4 (mod 6)\n    #   => l ≡ -1=5, 1, 3 (mod 6)\n    #   l in [1,5]: l=1,3,5 — 3 ways — matches.\n    #   So number of ways = number of b in {0,2,4} such that b != a (where a=1), which is 3 (all of them since a=1 not in {0,2,4}).\n    #   But earlier we said if a not in M, ways=count, but M={2,4} (excluding 0), and b=0 is also a solution.\n    #   We should include i=0 in the match count for the equation, but then exclude l=0.\n    \n    # Revised plan:\n    #   Let T = { i in [0, n-1] such that s[i:] + s[:i] = t }\n    #   Let c = |T|\n    #   For a rotation r = s rotated by a (i.e., r = s[a:] + s[:a]), the number of l in [1, n-1] such that rotation(r, l) = t is:\n    #       number of i in T such that (i - a) mod n != 0, i.e., i != a.\n    #   Because l = (i - a) mod n, and we need l in [1, n-1], so exclude l=0 which happens when i=a.\n    #   So if a in T, ways = c - 1\n    #   if a not in T, ways = c\n    #   But wait, if a in T, then i=a is in T, and l=0 for that i, so excluded, so c-1.\n    #   If a not in T, then no i equals a, so c ways.\n    \n    # In example 2: \n    #   T = {0,2,4}, c=3.\n    #   Initial a=0, so if a in T, ways to t = c-1 = 2 — matches (l=2,4).\n    #   From a non-t rotation, say a=1 not in T, ways = c = 3 — matches manual count.\n    #   From a=t state (a=2 or a=4), ways to t = c-1 = 2.\n    #   But are there two states at t? In terms of rotation index, yes, but the string is the same.\n    #   However, for counting ways, we don't care about the string, we care about the count of ways to reach t after k operations.\n    #   Let:\n    #       A_k = number of ways to be at a state with rotation index in T after k operations.\n    #       B_k = number of ways to be at a state with rotation index not in T after k operations.\n    #   But initially:\n    #       if 0 in T (i.e., s==t), then initial state has rotation index 0 in T, so A_0=1, B_0=0.\n    #       else, A_0=0, B_0=1.\n    #   Transitions:\n    #   From a state with rotation index a in T:\n    #       ways to go to a state in T: number of i in T such that (i - a) mod n in [1, n-1] and the resulting rotation index i is in T.\n    #       But the resulting rotation index after l is (a + l) mod n, and we want it in T.\n    #       l = (i - a) mod n for i in T.\n    #       l must be in [1, n-1], which is always true for i != a, so there are (c-1) such l that land in T.\n    #       ways to go to a state not in T: total_ops - (c-1) = (n-1) - (c-1) = n - c.\n    #   From a state with rotation index a not in T:\n    #       ways to go to a state in T: number of i in T, which is c (since a not in T, so all i give l !=0)\n    #       ways to go to a state not in T: (n-1) - c.\n    \n    # So:\n    #   A_k = A_{k-1} * (c-1) + B_{k-1} * c\n    #   B_k = A_{k-1} * (n - c) + B_{k-1} * (n - 1 - c)\n    \n    # This is a linear recurrence:\n    #   [A_k]   = [c-1    c    ] [A_{k-1}]\n    #   [B_k]     [n-c  n-1-c ] [B_{k-1}]\n    \n    # Let M = [[c-1, c], [n-c, n-1-c]]\n    # We need to compute M^k * [A0; B0]\n    \n    # Note: A0 = 1 if 0 in T else 0\n    # But 0 in T means s == t.\n    # And c = |T|, which we can compute via KMP.\n    \n    # Example 1: s=\"abcd\", t=\"cdab\", n=4\n    #   T = { i: s[i:]+s[:i] = \"cdab\" }\n    #   i=0: \"abcd\" != \"cdab\"\n    #   i=1: \"bcda\" != \"cdab\"\n    #   i=2: \"cdab\" = t -> yes\n    #   i=3: \"dabc\" != \"cdab\"\n    #   So T={2}, c=1.\n    #   A0 = 0 (since 0 not in T), B0=1.\n    #   Recurrence:\n    #       A_1 = 0*(1-1) + 1*1 = 1\n    #       B_1 = 0*(4-1) + 1*(4-1-1) = 2\n    #       A_2 = 1*(0) + 2*1 = 2\n    #       B_2 = 1*(3) + 2*(2) = 3+4=7 — but we don't care.\n    #   A_2=2 — matches.\n    \n    # Example 2: s=\"ababab\", t=\"ababab\", n=6\n    #   T = {0,2,4}, c=3.\n    #   A0 = 1 (0 in T), B0=0.\n    #   A_1 = 1*(3-1) + 0*3 = 2\n    #   B_1 = 1*(6-3) + 0 = 3\n    #   So for k=1, answer=2 — matches.\n    \n    # Now, k can be up to 1e15, so we need matrix exponentiation.\n    \n    # Steps:\n    # 1. Compute T: find all i in [0, n-1] such that s[i:]+s[:i] == t.\n    #    Use KMP on s+s to find occurrences of t, then take occurrences with start in [0, n-1].\n    # 2. c = number of such i.\n    # 3. A0 = 1 if 0 in T (i.e., s==t) else 0.\n    # 4. B0 = 1 - A0.\n    # 5. If k==0: return 1 if A0 else 0.\n    # 6. Use matrix exponentiation on the 2x2 matrix:\n    #        M = [[c-1, c],\n    #             [n-c, n-1-c]]\n    #    and multiply by vector [A0, B0]^T.\n    \n    # Note: All operations modulo MOD.\n    \n    # Build KMP for t in ss = s+s\n    ss = s + s\n    m = len(t)\n    # If t is not of length n, but problem says equal length, so m=n.\n    \n    # Edge: if t not in ss, then T is empty, c=0.\n    \n    # KMP failure function\n    fail = [0] * m\n    j = 0\n    for i in range(1, m):\n        while j > 0 and t[i] != t[j]:\n            j = fail[j-1]\n        if t[i] == t[j]:\n            j += 1\n        fail[i] = j\n    \n    # Find matches\n    j = 0\n    T_set = set()\n    for i in range(len(ss)):\n        while j > 0 and ss[i] != t[j]:\n            j = fail[j-1]\n        if ss[i] == t[j]:\n            j += 1\n        if j == m:\n            start = i - m + 1\n            if start < n:\n                T_set.add(start)\n            j = fail[j-1]\n    \n    c = len(T_set)\n    \n    # A0 = 1 if 0 in T_set else 0\n    A0 = 1 if 0 in T_set else 0\n    B0 = 1 - A0\n    \n    # If k==0, return A0\n    if k == 0:\n        return A0\n    \n    # Matrix M = [[c-1, c], [n-c, n-1-c]]\n    # But careful: n is len(s), and total operations = n-1.\n    \n    # Matrix multiplication for 2x2\n    def mat_mult(A, B):\n        a11 = (A[0][0]*B[0][0] + A[0][1]*B[1][0]) % MOD\n        a12 = (A[0][0]*B[0][1] + A[0][1]*B[1][1]) % MOD\n        a21 = (A[1][0]*B[0][0] + A[1][1]*B[1][0]) % MOD\n        a22 = (A[1][0]*B[0][1] + A[1][1]*B[1][1]) % MOD\n        return [[a11, a12], [a21, a22]]\n    \n    def mat_pow(matrix, power):\n        # Initialize result as identity\n        result = [[1, 0], [0, 1]]\n        base = matrix\n        while power:\n            if power & 1:\n                result = mat_mult(result, base)\n            base = mat_mult(base, base)\n            power //= 2\n        return result\n    \n    M = [[(c-1) % MOD, c % MOD], [(n - c) % MOD, (n - 1 - c) % MOD]]\n    M_k = mat_pow(M, k)\n    \n    # [A_k]   = M_k * [A0]\n    # [B_k]           [B0]\n    A_k = (M_k[0][0] * A0 + M_k[0][1] * B0) % MOD\n    return A_k\n```",
