      "generated_answer": "The point \\(D\\) is the intersection of the tangents to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(B\\) and \\(C\\). By the tangent-chord theorem, \\(\\angle DBC = \\angle BAC = A\\) and \\(\\angle DCB = \\angle CAB = A\\). Thus \\(\\triangle DBC\\) is isosceles with base angles equal to \\(A\\), which yields the tangent length\n\\[\nDB = DC = \\frac{a}{2\\cos A} = \\frac{9}{2\\cdot(11/25)} = \\frac{225}{22}.\n\\]\nBecause the tangent at \\(B\\) lies exterior to \\(\\angle ABC\\), the angle formed by lines \\(AB\\) and \\(DB\\) is \\(\\angle ABD = 180^\\circ - C\\). The law of cosines in \\(\\triangle ABD\\) therefore gives\n\\[\nAD^2 = AB^2 + DB^2 - 2\\cdot AB\\cdot DB\\cdot\\cos(180^\\circ-C) = \\frac{105625}{484},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nAD = \\frac{325}{22}.\n\\]\nThe line \\(DA\\) intersects \\(\\omega\\) again at \\(P\\), and the order of points on the line is \\(D\\)-\\(P\\)-\\(A\\). Consequently the power of \\(D\\) supplies the relation\n\\[\nDA\\cdot DP = DB^2.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(DP = DA - AP\\) produces\n\\[\nAP = DA - \\frac{DB^2}{DA} = \\frac{325}{22} - \\frac{50625}{484}\\cdot\\frac{22}{325} = \\frac{100}{13}.\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\nm+n = 100+13 = 113.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{113}",
