    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Both given logarithms equal 10, so\n\\[\nx\\log_x y=10\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4y\\log_y x=10.\n\\]\nLet \\(k=\\log_x y\\). The first identity immediately yields \\(k=10/x\\). Substituting into the second identity and using the reciprocal relation \\(\\log_y x=1/k\\) produces\n\\[\n\\frac{4y}{k}=10\\implies y=\\frac52k=\\frac{25}x.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nxy=x\\cdot\\frac{25}x=25.\n\\]\n(The auxiliary relation \\(y=x^{10/x}\\) is satisfied for a unique pair \\((x,y)\\) with both factors greater than 1, confirming that the value \\(xy=25\\) is attained.)\n\n\\boxed{25}",
