    "global index": "AIME_63",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Define $f(x)=|| x|-\\tfrac{{1}}{{2}}|$ and $g(x)=|| x|-\\tfrac{{1}}{{4}}|$. Find the number of intersections of the graphs of \\[y=4 g(f(\\sin (2 \\pi x))) \\quad\\text{{ and }}\\quad x=4 g(f(\\cos (3 \\pi y))).\\]\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The functions \\(F(x) = 4g(f(\\sin(2\\pi x)))\\) and \\(G(y) = 4g(f(\\cos(3\\pi y)))\\) both take values in \\([0,1]\\). Consequently all candidate intersection points lie in the unit square \\([0,1]\\times[0,1]\\).\n\nBoth \\(F\\) and \\(G\\) are obtained by composing the same piecewise-linear map\n\\[\nm(s)=4\\bigl|\\bigl||s|-1/2\\bigr|-1/4\\bigr|\n\\]\nwith a folded trigonometric function. The map \\(m\\) consists of four linear pieces on \\([0,1]\\), each of which is strictly monotonic and surjective onto \\([0,1]\\). Consequently \\(F\\) (respectively \\(G\\)) possesses exactly 16 (respectively 24) monotonic pieces on \\([0,1]\\).\n\nThe composition \\(q=F\\circ G\\) therefore inherits\n\\[\n24\\times16=384\n\\]\nmonotonic pieces. On each of these pieces \\(q\\) travels monotonically from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) or from \\(1\\) to \\(0\\). Hence the graph of \\(q\\) crosses the diagonal exactly once per piece, producing exactly 384 roots of the scalar equation\n\\[\nq(y)=y.\n\\]\nEach such root determines a unique abscissa \\(x=G(y)\\), and the resulting pair \\((x,y)\\) satisfies both original equations simultaneously. No other solutions exist outside the unit square.\n\nThus the two graphs intersect in precisely 384 points.\n\n\\boxed{384}",
