    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: For any finite set $X$, let $| X |$ denote the number of elements in $X$. Define\n\\[S_n = \\sum | A \\cap B | ,\\]\nwhere the sum is taken over all ordered pairs $(A, B)$ such that $A$ and $B$ are subsets of $\\left\\{{ 1 , 2 , 3,  \\cdots , n \\right\\}}$ with $|A| = |B|$.\nFor example, $S_2 = 4$ because the sum is taken over the pairs of subsets\n\\[(A, B) \\in \\left\\{{ (\\emptyset, \\emptyset) , ( \\{{1\\}} , \\{{1\\}} ), ( \\{{1\\}} , \\{{2\\}} ) , ( \\{{2\\}} , \\{{1\\}} ) , ( \\{{2\\}} , \\{{2\\}} ) , ( \\{{1 , 2\\}} , \\{{1 , 2\\}} ) \\right\\}} ,\\]\ngiving $S_2 = 0 + 1 + 0 + 0 + 1 + 2 = 4$.\nLet $\\frac{{S_{{2022}}{{S_{{2021}} = \\frac{{p}}{{q}}$, where $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find the remainder when $p + q$ is divided by\n1000.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We have  \n\\[\nS_n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k}^2 \\cdot k,\n\\]  \nbecause there are \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) ways to choose \\(A\\) of size \\(k\\) and \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) ways to choose \\(B\\) of size \\(k\\), and for fixed \\(A,B\\) of size \\(k\\), the intersection size \\(k\\) occurs only when \\(A=B\\), so the contribution is actually \\(\\binom{n}{k} \\cdot \\binom{n}{k} \\cdot \\frac{k}{?}\\)—more carefully:  \nFor each element, probability it lies in both is \\(\\frac{k}{n} \\cdot \\frac{k}{n}\\) times total pairs \\(\\binom{n}{k}^2\\), so  \n\\[\nS_n = \\sum_{k=0}^n \\binom{n}{k}^2 \\cdot k = n \\cdot 2^{2n-2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{?}\n\\]  \nActually known identity:  \n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^n k \\binom{n}{k}^2 = n \\binom{2n-1}{n-1}.\n\\]  \nThus  \n\\[\nS_n = n \\binom{2n-1}{n-1}.\n\\]  \nSo  \n\\[\n\\frac{S_{2022}}{S_{2021}} = \\frac{2022 \\binom{4043}{2021}}{2021 \\binom{4041}{2020}}.\n\\]  \nUsing \\(\\binom{4043}{2021} = \\frac{4043}{2022} \\binom{4042}{2021}\\) and \\(\\binom{4042}{2021} = \\frac{4042}{2021} \\binom{4041}{2020}\\) gives  \n\\[\n\\frac{2022}{2021} \\cdot \\frac{4043}{2022} \\cdot \\frac{4042}{2021} = \\frac{4043 \\cdot 4042}{2021^2}.\n\\]  \nSo \\(p = 4043 \\cdot 4042\\), \\(q = 2021^2\\). Compute mod 1000:  \n\\(4043 \\equiv 43\\), \\(4042 \\equiv 42\\), product \\(43\\cdot42 = 1806 \\equiv 806\\).  \n\\(2021^2 \\equiv 21^2 = 441\\).  \nThus \\(p+q \\equiv 806+441=1247 \\equiv 247 \\pmod{1000}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{247}\n\\]",
