    "global index": "AIME_89",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: There is a collection of $25$ indistinguishable white chips and $25$ indistinguishable black chips. Find the number of ways to place some of these chips in the $25$ unit cells of a $5\\times5$ grid such that: \n\neach cell contains at most one chip\nall chips in the same row and all chips in the same column have the same colour\nany additional chip placed on the grid would violate one or more of the previous two conditions.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The placements satisfying the uniformity conditions must be full rectangles of a single color, and distinct colors cannot share rows or columns. Thus any valid configuration is specified by (possibly empty) disjoint sets \\(R_w, R_b \\subseteq \\{1,\\dots,5\\}\\) and \\(C_w, C_b \\subseteq \\{1,\\dots,5\\}\\) together with the complete white rectangle on \\(R_w\\times C_w\\) and the complete black rectangle on \\(R_b\\times C_b\\).\n\nMaximality forbids empty cells that could receive a chip without violating uniformity. Consequently:\n- the all-white and all-black fillings of the entire grid are admissible;\n- every other maximal configuration must use both colors, which forces the partitions\n  \\[\n  R_w\\sqcup R_b=\\{1,\\dots,5\\},\\qquad C_w\\sqcup C_b=\\{1,\\dots,5\\}\n  \\]\n  with both blocks nonempty on each side.\n\nThere are precisely \\(2^5-2=30\\) ways to assign the rows to the two nonempty labeled blocks and likewise for the columns, producing\n\\[\n(2^5-2)^2+2=900+2=902\n\\]\ndistinct placements.\n\n\\boxed{902}",
