    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To count the subsets of \\(\\{1,2,\\dots,10\\}\\) with exactly one consecutive pair, first select the unique pair \\(\\{i,i+1\\}\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots,9\\). The two numbers adjacent to this pair (if they exist) must be excluded to avoid creating additional consecutive pairs. From the remaining available numbers, select any subset containing no two consecutive values.\n\nThe number of valid subsets of no consecutive integers that can be formed from a chain of length \\(r\\) is the Fibonacci number \\(F_{r+2}\\) (where \\(F_1=1\\), \\(F_2=1\\), \\(F_3=2,\\dots\\)). Accounting for the gaps created by the excluded numbers therefore yields the following counts for each \\(i\\):\n\n- \\(i=1,9\\): chain of length 7 gives \\(F_9=34\\) each;\n- \\(i=2,8\\): chain of length 6 gives \\(F_8=21\\) each;\n- \\(i=3,7\\): isolated singleton + chain of length 5 gives \\(2\\times F_7=26\\) each;\n- \\(i=4,6\\): chain of length 2 + chain of length 4 gives \\(F_4\\times F_6=24\\) each;\n- \\(i=5\\): two chains of length 3 gives \\(F_5\\times F_5=25\\).\n\nSumming these nine quantities produces the total\n\\[\n2(34+21+26+24)+25=235.\n\\]\nEach admissible subset is counted exactly once, since it contains precisely one consecutive pair.\n\n\\boxed{235}",
