    "global index": "AIME_79",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $\\omega\\neq 1$ be a 13th root of unity. Find the remainder when\n\\[\\prod_{{k=0}}^{{12}}(2-2\\omega^k+\\omega^{{2k}})\\]\nis divided by 1000.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The product in question may be rewritten as\n\\[\nP=\\prod_{\\substack{z^{13}=1}}(z^2-2z+2),\n\\]\nwhere the product runs over all thirteenth roots of unity \\(z\\). Factoring the quadratic gives\n\\[\nz^2-2z+2=(z-(1+i))(z-(1-i)),\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nP=\\Bigl(\\prod_z(z-(1+i))\\Bigr)\\Bigl(\\prod_z(z-(1-i))\\Bigr).\n\\]\nFor the monic polynomial \\(x^{13}-1=\\prod(x-z)\\) one has the identity\n\\[\n\\prod_z(z-a)=-(a^{13}-1)\n\\]\n(valid because the degree 13 is odd). Consequently\n\\[\nP=\\bigl((1+i)^{13}-1\\bigr)\\bigl((1-i)^{13}-1\\bigr).\n\\]\nExpressing \\(1+i=\\sqrt{2}\\,e^{i\\pi/4}\\) yields\n\\[\n(1+i)^{13}=-64(1+i),\n\\]\nand likewise for the conjugate. Therefore\n\\[\n(1+i)^{13}-1=-65-64i,\\qquad(1-i)^{13}-1=-65+64i,\n\\]\nand their product is\n\\[\nP=65^2+64^2=8321.\n\\]\nThe remainder of \\(8321\\) upon division by \\(1000\\) is consequently \\(321\\).\n\n\\boxed{321}",
