    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the grid entries be denoted\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\nA & B & C \\\\\n\\hline\nD & E & F \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwhere each letter is a digit from 0 to 9. The row-sum condition requires\n\\[\n100(A+D)+10(B+E)+(C+F)=999.\n\\]\nThe column-sum condition is\n\\[\n10(A+B+C)+(D+E+F)=99.\n\\]\nAddition of the two three-digit numbers with carries \\(C_1\\) (from units) and \\(C_2\\) (from tens) produces exactly four exhaustive cases according to the values of the carries. In every case except\n\\[\nC_1=C_2=0\n\\]\nthe implied value of the bottom-row digit sum exceeds 27 and is therefore impossible. The remaining case forces the pairwise relations\n\\[\nA+D=B+E=C+F=9\n\\]\ntogether with the auxiliary requirement\n\\[\nA+B+C=8.\n\\]\nSubstituting the pairwise relations into the column-sum equation confirms consistency. Consequently every triple of digits \\((A,B,C)\\) satisfying\n\\[\nA+B+C=8\n\\]\n(with each variable an integer between 0 and 9) determines a unique admissible grid. Since the largest variable can be at most 8, the upper-bound constraints are automatically satisfied; the number of non-negative integer solutions is therefore\n\\[\n\\binom{8+3-1}{2}=45.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{45}",
