    "global index": "AIME_76",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Let $\\triangle ABC$ have circumcenter $O$ and incenter $I$ with $\\overline{{IA}}\\perp\\overline{{OI}}$, circumradius $13$, and inradius $6$. Find $AB\\cdot AC$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Place \\(O\\) at the origin, so that \\(|\\overrightarrow{OA}|=R=13\\). The given conditions yield \\(OI^2=R(R-2r)=13\\), hence \\(OI=\\sqrt{13}\\). Since \\(\\overline{IA}\\perp\\overline{OI}\\), \\(\\triangle OAI\\) is right-angled at \\(I\\). Pythagoras therefore supplies\n\\[\nIA^2=OA^2-OI^2=169-13=156.\n\\]\nThe standard length formula \\(IA=r/\\sin(A/2)\\) now gives\n\\[\n\\sin(A/2)=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{156}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{13}.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\cos(A/2)=\\frac{\\sqrt{130}}{13},\\qquad\\sin A=2\\sin(A/2)\\cos(A/2)=\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{13}.\n\\]\nThe law of sines produces the side\n\\[\na=BC=2R\\sin A=4\\sqrt{30}.\n\\]\nThe half-angle formula \\(\\tan(A/2)=r/(s-a)\\) likewise yields\n\\[\ns-a=2\\sqrt{30},\n\\]\nso that\n\\[\ns=a+(s-a)=6\\sqrt{30}.\n\\]\nThe area is then\n\\[\n\\Delta=rs=36\\sqrt{30}.\n\\]\nFinally,\n\\[\nbc=AB\\cdot AC=\\frac{4R\\Delta}{a}=468.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{468}",
