    "global index": "AIME_62",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Each vertex of a regular octagon is independently colored either red or blue with equal probability. The probability that the octagon can then be rotated so that all of the blue vertices end up at positions where there were originally red vertices is $\\tfrac{{m}}{{n}}$, where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. What is $m+n$?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "grok-4-1-fast-reasoning",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The vertices of the octagon may be identified with the cyclic group \\(\\mathbb{Z}/8\\mathbb{Z}\\). A coloring corresponds to a subset \\(S \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z}/8\\mathbb{Z}\\) consisting of the blue vertices. The desired rotation exists if and only if the difference set \\(S-S\\) is a proper subset of \\(\\mathbb{Z}/8\\mathbb{Z}\\).\n\nConsequently it is enough to count the complementary collection of “bad” subsets (those for which \\(S-S=\\mathbb{Z}/8\\mathbb{Z}\\)) and subtract from the total number \\(2^8=256\\) of subsets. Subsets of cardinality at most 3 automatically fail to realize every difference, while every subset of cardinality at least 5 realizes every difference; there are exactly 93 subsets in each of these two ranges.\n\nIt remains only to count the 4-element subsets that realize every difference. There are \\(\\binom{8}{4}=70\\) subsets of size 4. Among them, the subsets that miss at least one nonzero difference are precisely the independent sets of size 4 in one of the four circulant graphs whose edges are the pairs differing by \\(\\pm1\\), \\(\\pm2\\), \\(\\pm3\\) or \\(4\\). These four quantities are respectively 2, 4, 2 and 16. All pairwise (and hence all higher) intersections of these families are empty. Inclusion-exclusion therefore yields exactly\n\\[\n2+4+2+16=24\n\\]\nsubsets of size 4 that miss at least one difference, and therefore exactly\n\\[\n70-24=46\n\\]\nsubsets of size 4 that realize every difference.\n\nAdding the 93 subsets of size greater than 4 shows that there are exactly 139 bad subsets. Hence there are\n\\[\n256-139=117\n\\]\ngood subsets, and the desired probability is \\(117/256\\). The fraction is already in lowest terms, so\n\\[\nm+n=117+256=373.\n\\]\n\n\\boxed{373}",
