    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The \\(2 \\times 2\\) grid has four inner edges whose colors may be chosen freely (in \\(2^4 = 16\\) ways). Label these edges \\(A, B, C, D \\in \\{0,1\\}\\) (with \\(1\\) denoting red). For each of the four squares the two inner edges determine an integer \\(k \\in \\{0,1,2\\}\\) equal to the number of red inner edges; exactly \\(2-k\\) of the square’s two outer edges must then be red, which can be done in\n\\[\nw(k) := \\binom{2}{2-k}\n\\]\nways. Equivalently,\n\\[\nw(k) = \n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }k=0\\text{ or }2,\\\\\n2 & \\text{if }k=1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe four values of \\(k\\) are \\(A+C\\), \\(B+C\\), \\(A+D\\) and \\(B+D\\), so the number of compatible outer-edge colourings for a fixed inner colouring is the product\n\\[\nw(A+C)\\,w(B+C)\\,w(A+D)\\,w(B+D).\n\\]\nSumming the product over all \\(16\\) assignments of the inner edges yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{A,B,C,D\\in\\{0,1\\}}w(A+C)w(B+C)w(A+D)w(B+D).\n\\]\nGrouping by the pair \\((C,D)\\) gives three cases:\n- \\((C,D)=(0,0)\\) contributes \\(25\\),\n- \\((C,D)=(1,1)\\) contributes \\(25\\),\n- the two mixed pairs \\((C,D)\\in\\{(0,1),(1,0)\\}\\) each contribute \\(16\\).\n\nThe total is therefore \\(25+25+32=82\\).\n\nThus the number of valid colourings is \\(82\\).\n\n\\boxed{82}",
