      "generated_answer": "Let \\(z = a + bi\\) where \\(a, b \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\) and \\(a^2 + b^2 = p\\). Then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Re}(z^3) = a(a^2 - 3b^2), \\qquad \\operatorname{Im}(z^3) = b(3a^2 - b^2).\n\\]\nThe values \\(p\\), \\(|\\operatorname{Re}(z^3)|\\) and \\(|\\operatorname{Im}(z^3)|\\) must satisfy the strict triangle inequalities. These inequalities hold precisely when\n\\[\n\\bigl||a(a^2-3b^2)| - |b(3a^2-b^2)|\\bigr| < p,\n\\]\nwhich occurs when the ratio \\(|a/b|\\) is close to the positive root \\(2 + \\sqrt{3} \\approx 3.732\\) of the associated cubic.\n\nThe only pairs of positive integers \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a^2 + b^2 < 1000\\) and ratio near \\(3.732\\) that yield a prime are \\((7,2)\\) (giving \\(p = 53\\)) and \\((15,4)\\) (giving \\(p = 241\\)). All larger candidate sums are composite, and all other primes \\(p < 1000\\) that are sums of two squares have ratios far from \\(3.732\\), producing differences larger than \\(p\\). Hence the largest admissible prime is \\(241\\).\n\n\\boxed{241}",
