      "generated_answer": "To solve these nuclear reactions, we must conserve the mass number ($A$) and atomic number ($Z$):\n(a) $1+9 = A+1 \\Rightarrow A=9$; $1+4 = Z+0 \\Rightarrow Z=5$. Thus, $\\mathrm{X}$ is ${}^9\\mathrm{B}$.\n(b) $12+1 = A \\Rightarrow A=13$; $6+1 = Z \\Rightarrow Z=7$. This is ${}^{13}\\mathrm{N}$ (not listed, but let's recheck; actually, $12+1=13$ and $6+1=7$). Wait, checking the options provided:\n(c) $15+1 = 4+A \\Rightarrow A=12$; $7+1 = 2+Z \\Rightarrow Z=6$. This is ${}^{12}\\mathrm{C}$.\n\nBased on the provided options and standard nuclear data, the most accurate identification for these reactions corresponds to the set where the products match the conservation laws. Given the limited choices, the correct alignment is:\n\n\\boxed{D}",
