    "global index": "MathQA_2482",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: what is the range of all the roots of | x ^ 2 - 3 | = x ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 4\nB. 3\nC. 2\nD. 1\nE. 0\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve $|x^2 - 3| = x$, we consider two cases: $x^2 - 3 = x$ (leading to $x^2 - x - 3 = 0$) and $x^2 - 3 = -x$ (leading to $x^2 + x - 3 = 0$). Solving these using the quadratic formula, we find the roots are $\\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}$ (with $\\frac{1+\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\approx 2.3$ and $\\frac{1-\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\approx -1.3$) and $\\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}$ (with $\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\approx 1.3$ and $\\frac{-1-\\sqrt{13}}{2} \\approx -2.3$). Since the absolute value must result in a non-negative $x$, the valid roots are $\\frac{1+\\sqrt{13}}{2}$ and $\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{13}}{2}$, and the range (difference) is 1.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
