    "global index": "MathQA_1395",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: if c and t are positive integers , ct + c + t can not be\n\nOptions: \nA. 5\nB. 6\nC. 7\nD. 8\nE. 9\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We rewrite $ ct + c + t = (c+1)(t+1) - 1 $. Let $ x = (c+1)(t+1) $, so $ ct + c + t = x - 1 $. Since $ c, t \\geq 1 $, then $ c+1 \\geq 2 $, $ t+1 \\geq 2 $, so $ x \\geq 4 $. Thus, $ ct + c + t = x - 1 \\geq 3 $, but more importantly, $ x $ must be a composite integer (product of two integers ≥2). So $ ct + c + t + 1 $ must be composite. Then $ ct + c + t $ cannot be such that $ (ct + c + t + 1) $ is prime. Check values:\n\n- If $ ct + c + t = 5 $, then $ 6 $ is composite → possible.\n- $ =6 $, then $ 7 $ is prime → impossible.\n- $ =7 $, then $ 8 $ composite → possible.\n- $ =8 $, $ 9 $ composite → possible.\n- $ =9 $, $ 10 $ composite → possible.\n\nOnly when result is 6 does $ ct + c + t + 1 = 7 $, a prime, which cannot be expressed as $ (c+1)(t+1) $ with integers ≥2. So 6 is impossible.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
